# Binomial exact test DGE — three tissue comparisons (BixBench bix-3-q5)

## Answer

**Final blood vs Baseline blood shows negligible effects.** It is the lowest of the three
comparisons on *every* metric, in *every* one of the 4 analysis variants × 2 p0 constructions
(8/8 cells), under both the BH-FDR and the raw-p decision rule.

## Method (as pre-registered and approved)

Counts: `NormCount` sheet, 25,402 genes × 90 samples (positional cols 2–91 only; cols 92–187 are
the deposition's own derived Max/Median/DESeq blocks and were excluded). Groups of 30/30/30
taken from `Mapping_statistics` `Tissue`/`Code`, not from cosmetic row-1 headers or sample-ID suffixes.

Per gene, per contrast: `k = round(Σ arm A counts)`, `n = k + round(Σ arm B counts)` (pooling the
30 replicates), two-sided **central exact binomial test** (R `binom.test` convention, computed in
log space via `binom.logcdf/logsf`; implementation verified to 4e-15 against `scipy`/`binom.cdf`
on 500 random cases), tested against the library-share null `p0 = N_A/(N_A+N_B)` with N summed over
the matrix **with the top-100 genes trimmed** (primary). Genes zero in both arms are dropped.
Note: NormCount values are **size-factor normalised and fractional** (68.1% non-integer, all on a
0.01 grid) — so this is a model **approximation, not an exact test on true discrete counts**.

